LORE.push(
  {ch:337, n:'The Dragonvale Request', t:'Dragonvale asked the Imperial Guard to investigate irregular military provisioning on its western frontier: delays, mismatched supplies and diverted goods, with the Dee household named.'},
  {ch:338, n:'The General\'s Daughter', t:'Delilah, now composed and capable, was already reviewing the western frontier accounts. She found inconsistent quantities, missing acknowledgments, inflated prices and units reported short, and believes goods meant for the western garrisons are being diverted.'},
  {ch:339, n:'Numbers Before Swords', t:'Supplies approved, paid for and dispatched never reached several frontier garrisons: grain 10,000 purchased, 6,200 delivered; winter clothing 4,000 and 800. The same merchants, warehouses and intermediaries recur: an organised network.'},
  {ch:340, n:'Damien Dee', t:'Delilah\'s older brother: decorated, humble and courteous, firm but approachable, and a listener who treats Jade as an equal. A military commander of the Dee household.'},
  {ch:340, n:'The Dee Household', t:'A well-established family team, with each member filling a clear role in military, logistics and regional affairs. Its influence and resources reach the western frontier.'},
  {ch:341, n:'Independent Legal Standing', t:'Damien earned substantial military merit in the western campaign and is considering using part of it to secure independent legal standing for Delilah, so she can manage her own holdings, contracts and people openly. Conservative factions may resist.'},
  {ch:342, n:'The Missing Convoys', t:'Convoys left Dragonvale on paper and never arrived, or arrived with a fraction of their cargo. At a rest station crates held stone and scrap and the seals were high-quality copies. The supplies are diverted to hidden warehouses by a network with access to official supplies.'},
  {ch:343, n:'Ink and Grain', t:'Forged signatures and copied seals made the shipments look legal; the quantities themselves were impossible. The paperwork was forged and the numbers inflated to move the real supplies elsewhere: a coordinated scheme.'},
  {ch:344, n:'The Woman Jade Remembered', t:'Working alone, Jade and Delilah spoke of the past. Delilah acknowledged that she had been arrogant, impatient and too quick to judge, and had treated Jade unfairly; Jade said they cannot change the past but can work properly now.'},
  {ch:345, n:'No Excuses', t:'Delilah gave Jade the first real apology: she was jealous, proud, arrogant and at times cruel, and blamed neither Roc, court custom nor youth. Jade said she sees it is sincere but cannot pretend everything is fine. She is not ready to forgive yet; it is a beginning.'},
  {ch:346, n:'The Contract House', t:'A house of military suppliers using old noble connections to secure front contracts, then redirecting supplies to private buyers and hidden routes. One supplier appears under three different names with an identical seal.'},
  {ch:347, n:'The Qilang Tribes', t:'An isolated tribal region on the western frontier where the missing convoys were last seen. Damien took part in the western campaign against the tribes, and the name still silences him: it is a reminder of losses, difficult choices and a part of the war he has not spoken about.'},
  {ch:348, n:'The Woman Who Found Him', t:'A woman of the Qilang Tribes found the wounded Damien, hid him, treated his wounds with herbs and guided him home through the mountains, asking nothing. He has not seen her since. Her name is not known.'}
);

