LORE.push(
  {ch:493, n:'Crown Prince, Temporary Party Member', t:'Aster joined the Crimson Guard as a temporary guest unit without replacing Sally. Jade reluctantly took responsibility for training her young brother-in-law in real field work, and the six set out together.'},
  {ch:494, n:'Court Training Is Not This', t:'Travelling with Jade\'s party, Aster found dirt, improvisation, civilians and changing plans. He helped the villagers, lifted barrels and ate rough food, and began to understand that leadership is about people, choices and being ready to adapt.'},
  {ch:495, n:'Sister-in-Law\'s Rules', t:'Jade gave Aster five practical rules: people first, be prepared, plans change, help when you can, and take care of yourself and your team. Devon enjoyed watching his brother learn what serving under Jade means, and Aster accepted that he could see why they matter.'},
  {ch:496, n:'Lady Elise Valen', t:'At the frontier the party reunited with Lady Elise Valen, one of Aster\'s former Crown Princess candidates and the finalist he chose. She shared supply routes, safe villages and trusted clans, warned of the northern pass, and rejoined as a trusted ally whose frontier knowledge became essential.'},
  {ch:497, n:'The Frontier Is People', t:'Elise showed Aster what no court map can teach: a border is people, work, grain and trade, and a careless order from afar can become a season of hardship. For Aster the frontier began to change from territory into responsibility.'},
  {ch:498, n:'Lady Yulan Fen\'s Records', t:'Fen joined the investigation as a government scholar and showed that several supposedly old frontier claims and agreements had been recently altered: wording, dates, seals and clauses changed, permits backdated, favouring certain families over the local clans. The party would compare archives and gather the people\'s testimonies.'},
  {ch:499, n:'Scholar and Frontier Lady', t:'Lady Yulan Fen and Lady Elise Valen worked extremely well together: Fen read the written law and records, Elise knew the customs, routes and people. Their friendship began, and gave the investigation a clearer and more balanced view of the frontier.'},
  {ch:500, n:'The Prince Who Likes the Scholar', t:'While Fen presented her analysis of the frontier records, the party saw plainly that Aster admires her. Devon noted that Fen\'s family wants influence while Aster wants the woman, and those are not the same; Jade understood that his heart had already chosen the scholar.'},
  {ch:501, n:'The Man Elise Never Forgot', t:'Elise told Jade of Tarin Ash, a childhood companion who left on a mission beyond the northern pass and never returned. She did not ask Jade to find him, only to know whether he survived: she would rather know the truth, even if painful, than live in uncertainty. Jade said she would see what they could find out.'},
  {ch:502, n:'The Valen Question', t:'Sky noticed Elise\'s surname and checked the old Valen records: the family split into branches over generations, and Elise\'s western frontier branch and his mother\'s southern branch may share a distant ancestor. The records were too old to be certain, but gave Sky another piece of his maternal history, and a starting point.'},
  {ch:503, n:'The Road of Regret', t:'Along the road the travellers began to see people and futures they lost: Elise saw Tarin, Aster saw a life where he abandoned the throne for Fen. Jade recognised the visions as an external influence, a place, artifact or magic along the route that draws out regrets and lost choices, and warned that more would come.'}
);
